UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 5, Lesson 1: Making an artwork about our place', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"An artwork about a place shows what makes that place special: the shapes of its buildings or plants, its colours, its textures and the feelings it gives you. Artists plan by choosing a theme, drawing small thumbnail sketches and deciding which elements will show the mood. They decide on a foreground, middle ground and background, so objects near the viewer look larger and lower on the page and far objects look smaller and higher. Choose what to leave out as well as what to include.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same arts idea to a new artwork or performance, then compare two choices for clarity of effect.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nYou want an artwork about your street. Name the place, one thing for the foreground and one for the background, and say which colours show the mood.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''My street: a big front gate in the foreground, houses in the middle and small trees far away. Warm yellows and soft greens make a friendly, quiet mood.'' Large things in front and small things behind show distance, and the colours support the mood.\n\nWATCH FOR\nBelieving that you must include everything you see. Crowding in everything makes the artwork confusing, so artists choose the most important parts of the place that show its character.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nPlan an artwork about a place you know well. Draw three thumbnail sketches, choose one and mark the foreground, middle ground and background. Make the final artwork using at least two elements from this unit (line, shape, colour, texture or pattern), then write a short artist statement about your choices.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one artistic choice you changed and why. Movement or instrument work is optional, adult-supervised and can be described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Creative Arts","learning_intention":"An artwork about a place shows what makes that place special: the shapes of its buildings or plants, its colours, its textures and the feelings it gives you. Artists plan by choosing a theme, drawing small thumbnail sketches and deciding which elements will show the mood. They decide on a foreground, middle ground and background, so objects near the viewer look larger and lower on the page and far objects look smaller and higher. Choose what to leave out as well as what to include.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Explore and learn","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: scrap paper, crayons, safe classroom instruments or clear movement space with an adult nearby"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["CA2-VIS-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Plan an artwork about a place you know well. Draw three thumbnail sketches, choose one and mark the foreground, middle ground and background. Make the final artwork using at least two elements from this unit (line, shape Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using a theme on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Believing that you must include everything you see.. Correction: Strong answer: ''My street: a big front gate in the foreground, houses in the middle and small trees far away.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Believing that you must include everything you see. Crowding in everything makes the artwork confusing, so artists choose the most important parts of the place that show its character. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating a theme on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with a theme, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about a theme with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for a theme. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for a theme. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material.","A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson.","''My street: a big front gate in the foreground, houses in the middle and small trees far away.","Believing that you must include everything you see."],"question":"You want an artwork about your street. Name the place, one thing for the foreground and one for the background, and say which colours show the mood. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for a theme; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Believing that you must include everything you see.","An artwork about a place shows what makes that place special: the shapes of its buildings or plants, its colours, its","''My street: a big front gate in the foreground, houses in the middle and small trees far away.","Careful use of a plan and explain why it matters with clear evidence."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with a theme?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named a plan and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name a plan and explain why it matters only, without linking it to a theme.","Apply a theme to new material and name the a plan and explain why it matters used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Believing that you must include everything you see."],"question":"Which response best applies a theme with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses elements, techniques and audience effect appropriately for this lesson on a theme. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance.","Believing that you must include everything you see.","''My street: a big front gate in the foreground, houses in the middle and small trees far away.","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named."],"question":"Which response best uses elements, techniques and audience effect for a theme?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Believing that you must include everything you see.","Name a specific element or technique and say what effect it creates for a theme.","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance."],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about a theme?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Believing that you must include everything you see.","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance.","Example: Lena makes an artwork of her local park."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — An artwork about a place shows what makes that place special: the shapes of its buildings or — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster.","Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue.","Name the artistic choice, its effect, and keep cultural protocols respectful.","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete."],"question":"Which action keeps elements, techniques and audience effect accurate for a theme?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Retype the worked example with no new example.","Believing that you must include everything you see.","Name a plan and explain why it matters only, with no linked example.","Plan an artwork about a place you know well."],"question":"Which independent response best applies a theme to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about a theme with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating a theme on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using a theme on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for a theme. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with a theme, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Believing that you must include everything you see.. Correction: Strong answer: ''My street: a big front gate in the foreground, houses in the middle and small trees far away.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent artwork or performance notes and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-arts-y3-w05-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA Creative Arts alignment using catalogue outcome codes (CA2-*); content-point mapping remains pending. First Nations arts content requires local Aboriginal educator or Custodian guidance before acceptance; do not invent sacred designs.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise a theme.","Read the mission before you start making or performing.","Keep the audience effect of your choice in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not name an effect clearly."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the element or technique that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore elements, safety or respectful practice.","Prefer a named artistic reason over a guess that merely looks colourful."],"submit":["Confirm that your choice and reason are both present.","Proofread element names and safety notes.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact element, technique or cue before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key artistic choices.","Say what each choice does for the artwork or audience.","A final praise word alone is weaker than a named effect."],"teach2":["Compare a weak choice with a stronger one.","Change the element or technique, not only the decoration.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh subject or piece that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original work notes and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill on new material."]},"subject":"Visual Arts","success_criteria":["I can use a theme with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify a plan and explain why it matters.","I can practise a sketch independently on a new prompt.","I can notice a place needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"An artwork about a place shows what makes that place special: the shapes of its buildings or plants, its colours, its textures and the feelings it gives you.","options":["A guess with no artistic reason.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An artwork about a place shows what makes that place special: the shapes of its buildings or plants, its colours, its","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about a theme with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Lena makes an artwork of her local park. Her thumbnail sketches show a big gum tree in the foreground, the play equipment in the middle ground and tiny hills at the back. She chooses warm greens and yellows for a happy mood. Each shape is chosen to say ''sunny, busy park''.\n\nTRY FIRST\nYou want an artwork about your street. Name the place, one thing for the foreground and one for the background, and say which colours show the mood.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''My street: a big front gate in the foreground, houses in the middle and small trees far away. Warm yellows and soft greens make a friendly, quiet mood.'' Large things in front and small things behind show distance, and the colours support the mood.","explain":"An artwork about a place shows what makes that place special: the shapes of its buildings or plants, its colours, its textures and the feelings it gives you. Artists plan by choosing a theme, drawing small thumbnail sketches and deciding which elements will show the mood. They decide on a foreground, middle ground and background, so objects near the viewer look larger and lower on the page and far objects look smaller and higher. Choose what to leave out as well as what to include.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Believing that you must include everything you see. Crowding in everything makes the artwork confusing, so artists choose the most important parts of the place that show its character. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Foreground and background size.","caption":"Near is big, far is small.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Foreground</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">large gate and tree</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Background</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">small hills</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example: Lena makes an artwork of her local park. Her thumbnail sketches show a big gum tree in the foreground, the play Then write your own artistic reason.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.\nJuno: Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example: Lena makes an artwork of her local park. Her thumbnail sketches show a big gum tree in the foreground, the play Then write your own artistic reason."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect. Apply the lesson idea with evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason.","Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about a theme with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Lena makes an artwork of her local park. Her thumbnail sketches show a big gum tree in the foreground, the play equipment in the middle ground and tiny hills at the back. She chooses warm greens and yellows for a happy mood. Each shape is chosen to say ''sunny, busy park''. Look at each line, sound, movement or role choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key artistic choices from the example and say what each one does for the audience or artwork.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful response names the element or technique and its effect, not only “it looks nice”.","explain":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final praise word without naming a choice does not show arts thinking. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Show the artistic choice","visual":[{"alt":"Planning with thumbnails.","caption":"Sketch, then choose.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Thumbnails</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">three small sketches</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Final</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">best one enlarged</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison.","options":["A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason.","Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about a theme with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nBelieving that you must include everything you see. Crowding in everything makes the artwork confusing, so artists choose the most important parts of the place that show its character. Here is a more helpful example: Example: Lena makes an artwork of her local park. Her thumbnail sketches show a big gum tree in the foreground, the play equipment in the middle ground and tiny hills at the back. She chooses warm greens and yellows for a happy mood. Each shape is chosen to say ''sunny, busy park''.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice weakens the work, then write a stronger choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''My street: a big front gate in the foreground, houses in the middle and small trees far away. Warm yellows and soft greens make a friendly, quiet mood.'' Large things in front and small things behind show distance, and the colours support the mood.","explain":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the decoration without changing the artistic idea does not fix the error. Believing that you must include everything you see. Crowding in everything makes the artwork confusing, so artists choose the most important parts of the place that show its character. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example.","options":["Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about a theme with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nAn artwork about a place shows what makes that place special: the shapes of its buildings or plants, its colours, its textures and the feelings it gives you. Artists plan by choosing a theme, drawing small thumbnail sketches and deciding which elements will show the mood. They decide on a foreground, middle ground and background, so objects near the viewer look larger and lower on the page and far objects look smaller and higher. Choose what to leave out as well as what to include. Now choose a different subject or piece to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same arts idea as the model, but with different content or material.","explain":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new artwork or performance. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Transfer to a new artwork or performance"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 5, Lesson 1: Making an artwork about our place","unit_number":1,"unit_title":"Drawing, line, shape and colour","week":5,"week_in_unit":5,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: Lena makes an artwork of her local park. Her thumbnail sketches show a big gum tree in the foreground, the play equipment in the middle ground and tiny hills at the back. She chooses warm greens and yellows for a happy mood. Each shape is chosen to say ''sunny, busy park''.\n\nWrite what makes this example effective, naming the element, technique or performance choice and its effect.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Foreground and background size.","caption":"Near is big, far is small.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Foreground</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">large gate and tree</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Background</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">small hills</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Planning with thumbnails.","caption":"Sketch, then choose.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Thumbnails</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">three small sketches</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Final</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">best one enlarged</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 3"}') WHERE seed_key='s2-arts-y3-w05-l1' AND family_id IS NULL;
